course: dictionary
lesson: 4852
title: Hyperbolic language: big claims
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Hyperbolic language exaggerates on purpose, to make something sound much bigger, better or worse than it really is. Nobody expects it to be literally true. «I'm so hungry I could eat a horse» is hyperbolic: the speaker is not going to eat a horse. They just want you to know they are very hungry.
explain: Check the numbers and the words with care. In «Gran has told me a thousand times she was born in 1950», the year 1950 is real, but «a thousand times» is hyperbolic, because she has not really said it a thousand times. Ask yourself: could this really be true? If not, it may be hyperbole.
explain: Adverts and news often use hyperbolic claims, like «the best ever!», to make you want something. A good reader notices the big words and asks whether the claim is fair. Writers also use hyperbolic language in stories and jokes, because it is fun and gives strong effects.
fact: Hyperbolic means exaggerating on purpose, to make something sound much bigger, better or worse than it really is.
fact: «I'm so hungry I could eat a horse» is a hyperbolic sentence.
fact: Hyperbolic language is used for effect or fun, not to trick people.
fact: In maths, a hyperbola is a special open curve with two separate branches, so hyperbolic can also mean to do with a hyperbola.
exercise: In «Oh yeah, I've ONLY told you a million times», which part is hyperbolic? | you | *a million | ONLY | Oh yeah
exercise: In «Gran has told me a thousand times she was born in 1950», which part is hyperbolic? | 1950 | *a thousand times | Gran | she was born
exercise: An advert says, «The best pizza ever!» What kind of claim is this? | A maths curve | *A hyperbolic claim, an exaggeration to sell the pizza | A true measurement of taste | A quiet whisper
test: Which sentence uses hyperbolic language? | The bag is quite heavy | *This bag weighs a million tonnes | The meeting starts at nine
test: In maths, what is a hyperbola? | A small round hill | *A special open curve with two separate branches | A sort of exaggerated story | A type of biscuit
exam: Why should a reader notice hyperbolic claims in adverts? | *It helps you spot exaggerations that may not be true | It proves the product is free | It means the advert is from a maths book | It means the advert is in a foreign language
